## Proof

**Setup.** We have 121 points in a square of side 10 km (area $S = 100\text{ km}^2$). We seek the largest $A$ such that every configuration must contain three points forming a triangle of area $\le A$.

**Triangulation of the convex hull.** Let the 121 points have $h$ vertices on their convex hull ($3 \le h \le 121$). A triangulation of the point set (triangulating the convex hull using all 121 points as vertices) produces

$$T = 2n - 2 - h$$

triangles, where $n = 121$. This is the standard Euler-formula count: every interior point added to a triangulation increases the triangle count by 2, starting from $h-2$ for the hull alone.

**Area bound via pigeonhole.** The $T$ triangles partition the convex hull, so their total area equals the area of the convex hull, which is at most the area of the square:

$$\sum_{i=1}^{T} [\text{area of triangle } i] \le 100.$$

By the pigeonhole principle, at least one of these $T$ triangles has area

$$\le \frac{100}{T} = \frac{100}{2n-2-h}.$$

Since each triangulation triangle is determined by three of the 121 beacons, this gives a valid triple with area $\le \frac{100}{2n-2-h}$.

**Worst case over $h$.** The guarantee must hold for *every* configuration, so we take the worst case — the configuration that makes the bound as large as possible. Since $\frac{100}{2n-2-h}$ is increasing in $h$, the worst case is $h = n = 121$ (all points on the convex hull), giving

$$A = \frac{100}{2(121)-2-121} = \frac{100}{242-2-121} = \frac{100}{119}.$$

**Tightness.** Place all 121 points on the boundary of the square so that they form a convex 121-gon whose area equals $100\text{ km}^2$ (the full square). Any triangulation of this convex 121-gon yields exactly $121 - 2 = 119$ triangles whose areas sum to $100$. Thus the average triangle area is exactly $\frac{100}{119}$, and no configuration can force the minimum triangle to be smaller than what the pigeonhole bound permits; the bound $\frac{100}{119}$ is achieved in the limit by configurations where the 119 triangulation triangles have nearly equal area.

**Conclusion.** The maximum threshold is

$$\boxed{A = \dfrac{100}{119}\text{ km}^2}.$$

